WEBVTT

00:00:01.120 --> 00:00:13.740
<v Ira Glass>Allen is not the real Santa Claus-- the guy in the North Pole. Obviously-- and I say this to any little kids who might be listening to me right now-- that guy in the North Pole is amazing. Allen is not that guy.
<v Ira Glass>艾伦并不是真正的圣诞老人——也就是住在北极的那个人。显然——我得对可能正在收听节目的小朋友们说——北极的那位是真的很棒，但艾伦不是他。

00:00:13.740 --> 00:00:26.260
<v Ira Glass>Allen's just a guy who dresses up as Santa sometimes. Allen doesn't have Santa's flying reindeer. Allen doesn't have legions of elves backing him up. And most of all, Allen doesn't have his years of experience.
<v Ira Glass>艾伦只是一个偶尔打扮成圣诞老人模样的人。他没有圣诞老人的飞行驯鹿，没有成群结队的精灵协助，最重要的是，他没有圣诞老人那多年的经验。

00:00:26.260 --> 00:00:32.460
<v Allen>This is my first year as Santa, my first-- oh, I'm going the wrong way.
<v Allen>这是我第一年当圣诞老人，我第一次……哦，我走错路了。

00:00:32.460 --> 00:00:34.970
<v Ira Glass>He's driving to his very first chair gig.
<v Ira Glass>他正开车去参加他的第一份“坐镇”工作。

00:00:34.970 --> 00:00:44.940
<v Allen>OK. I'm so nervous. I-- [BREATHES] I'm nervous. I'm really, really nervous.
<v Allen>好吧，我太紧张了。我……[深呼吸]我很紧张。我真的、真的很紧张。

00:00:44.940 --> 00:00:56.550
<v Ira Glass>A chair gig-- you sit in a chair, talk to kids about what they want for Christmas. One of our producers, Aviva DeKornfeld, rode with him to this job. This was in Redlands, California, an hour or so east of Los Angeles.
<v Ira Glass>所谓“坐镇”工作，就是坐在椅子上，听孩子们说说他们想要的圣诞礼物。我们的制作人之一，阿维瓦·德科恩菲尔德（Aviva DeKornfeld），陪他一起去执行这项工作。地点是在加利福尼亚州的雷德兰兹，距离洛杉矶大约一小时车程。

00:00:56.550 --> 00:01:15.990
<v Ira Glass>Allen is an earnest guy with a kind face, who-- and he would tell you this himself-- tends to overthink things a little. Really, he told Aviva, he's not sure what to expect at this gig. Like, what could go wrong? So he's just been trying to prepare for everything by making some lists, checking them twice.
<v Ira Glass>艾伦是个真诚的人，面相和蔼，而且——他自己也会这么说——他倾向于把事情想得太复杂。他告诉阿维瓦，他确实不知道该期待什么。比如，会出什么岔子？所以他一直在列清单，并反复检查，试图为一切做好准备。

00:01:15.990 --> 00:01:27.690
<v Allen>Last night, I made checklists on my phone for all the things that I would need and all the things I would potentially need. I have a backup for almost everything that I wear.
<v Allen>昨天晚上，我在手机上列了清单，记下了我需要的所有东西，以及所有我可能需要的东西。我穿的几乎每一件东西都有备份。

00:01:27.690 --> 00:01:28.800
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>How come?
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>为什么？

00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:51.930
<v Allen>Well, in case a baby spits up, I need another shirt. If the coat gets dirty. I need another coat. And I have to have extra gloves, and I've been kind of manic about trying to anticipate all the catastrophes that could happen, one way or another, and prepare for them.
<v Allen>嗯，万一孩子吐在身上，我需要换一件衬衫。如果外套脏了，我需要另一件外套。我还得有额外的手套。为了预见可能发生的各种灾难，我简直有点疯狂了，总想着要未雨绸缪。

00:01:51.930 --> 00:01:57.680
<v Ira Glass>He's also worried about his beard. He's 53, so his natural beard is still mostly brown.
<v Ira Glass>他也担心他的胡子。他53岁了，所以他自然的胡子大部分还是棕色的。

00:01:57.680 --> 00:02:07.520
<v Allen>So I started bleaching my beard in June, and my beard started getting smaller. The bleach was damaging it.
<v Allen>所以我从六月就开始漂白胡子，结果胡子开始变少了。漂白剂损伤了它。

00:02:07.520 --> 00:02:08.750
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>So it was breaking off?
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>所以它断掉了？

00:02:08.750 --> 00:02:10.730
<v Allen>Yeah.
<v Allen>对。

00:02:10.730 --> 00:02:23.660
<v Ira Glass>He's tried some expensive hair products to stop the damage, but it's still a little shorter than he wants. And maybe his greatest anxiety going into this gig was the questions that the kids might ask him. Would he say the right thing?
<v Ira Glass>他试过一些昂贵的护发产品来修复损伤，但胡子还是比他想要的短了一些。而且，他这次工作最大的焦虑可能在于孩子们会问他什么问题。他能说出合适的话吗？

00:02:23.660 --> 00:02:49.310
<v Allen>There might be a child with a real problem, like their mom is gone, or somebody is incarcerated, or something like that. And I can't-- I can't bring anyone back that they've lost, and I really worry about being asked about that. I can't bring someone home who's on a deployment somewhere.
<v Allen>可能会有孩子遇到真正的问题，比如他们的妈妈不在了，或者有人入狱了之类的。我无法……我无法让他们失去的人回来，我真的很担心被问到这些。我也无法让那些去前线服役的人回家。

00:02:49.310 --> 00:03:04.760
<v Ira Glass>He frets and drives on his way to the gig, two full Santa's costumes in the bag. You know, Christmas comes around every year, always the same-- with the same songs, and the same stage props, and same specials on TV. You can get numb to it.
<v Ira Glass>他带着两套完整的圣诞老人服装，一路焦虑地开向目的地。你知道，圣诞节每年都会到来，总是千篇一律——同样的歌曲、同样的舞台道具、电视上播放同样的特别节目。人对这些很容易变得麻木。

00:03:04.760 --> 00:03:18.190
<v Ira Glass>It's so easy to lose the magical feeling that so many people had as kids around the holiday. But one thing you can say about Allen-- he's not feeling numb. Just the opposite. He is on high alert.
<v Ira Glass>人们在童年时期感受到的那种节日魔力，长大后很容易丢失。但关于艾伦，有一点可以肯定——他并没有感到麻木。恰恰相反，他处于高度戒备状态。

00:03:18.190 --> 00:03:31.060
<v Ira Glass>Not just because he wants to be a perfect Santa for these little kids. Mainly, he's that way because it is his first time in the chair. And there are all kinds of reasons that is a big deal for him, that we're going to get into in a minute or two.
<v Ira Glass>这不仅是因为他想在孩子们面前做一个完美的圣诞老人，主要是因为这是他第一次坐在那把椅子上。这对他来说意义重大，原因有很多，我们稍后会详细聊聊。

00:03:31.060 --> 00:03:50.710
<v Ira Glass>But my point right now is, when you're trying something for the first time, you can just taste all the potential and excitement of what it could be. You feel feelings. Which is why this Christmas, here at our show, we thought it would be nice to spend an hour with people who are not doing what they've ever done in the past at Christmas, who are not doing the same old, same old.
<v Ira Glass>但我想说的是，当你第一次尝试某件事时，你能品味到它所有的潜力和可能性。你会产生各种感受。这就是为什么在这个圣诞节，我们的节目想花一个小时，和那些在圣诞节尝试新鲜事、拒绝陈词滥调的人在一起。

00:03:50.710 --> 00:04:10.440
<v Ira Glass>We want to spend the hour with people who are trying something brand new at Christmas, because they have a shot at some deep Christmas-y feeling. From WBEZ Chicago, it's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. Stay with us.
<v Ira Glass>我们想和那些在圣诞节尝试全新事物的人共度这段时间，因为他们有机会获得某种深刻的圣诞感。这里是芝加哥 WBEZ 电台的《美国生活》（This American Life），我是艾拉·格拉斯（Ira Glass）。请继续收听。

00:04:10.440 --> 00:04:23.880
<v Ira Glass>Act One, Does Santa Believe in Himself? OK, so Allen-- or maybe I should say Santa Allen-- is on his way to his first chair gig, as we said. Producer Aviva DeKornfeld went with him to see how he would do on that gig.
<v Ira Glass>第一幕，圣诞老人相信他自己吗？好吧，如我们所说，艾伦——或者我该叫他圣诞老人艾伦——正在前往他第一份“坐镇”工作的路上。制作人阿维瓦·德科恩菲尔德陪他一起去，看看他表现如何。

00:04:23.880 --> 00:04:35.100
<v Ira Glass>But to understand why he is so nervous and what he hoped to get out of the gig, you kind of have to understand what the job means for him in the first place. And so that is where Aviva is going to start.
<v Ira Glass>但要理解他为什么如此紧张，以及他希望从这份工作中获得什么，你得先了解这份工作对他来说意味着什么。这就是阿维瓦要开始讲述的地方。

00:04:35.100 --> 00:04:50.280
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>Before getting into the Santa business, Allen had a job that was sort of the opposite of being Santa. He was a corrections officer at a state prison. He'd gotten into the work because he needed the money and stability, and stayed for over two decades.
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>在进入圣诞老人这一行之前，艾伦从事的工作简直与圣诞老人截然相反。他在一所州立监狱当惩教官。他做这份工作是因为他需要钱和稳定，一干就是二十多年。

00:04:50.280 --> 00:05:06.630
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>But he hated it, and felt increasingly queasy about the whole system and his role in it. And then, in 2017, he got a break from it, though not under great conditions. He got really sick and ended up having to take nearly a year and a half off from work for medical leave.
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>但他讨厌这份工作，对整个制度以及自己在其中的角色感到越来越恶心。后来在2017年，他终于得以脱身，尽管环境并不理想。他病得很重，最终不得不请了近一年半的病假。

00:05:06.630 --> 00:05:16.590
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>He got depressed, stopped shaving, grew a long beard. One day, to cheer himself up, Allen and his wife, Erin, decided to go to a comic book convention.
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>他陷入了抑郁，不再刮胡子，留起了长长的胡子。有一天，为了让自己振作起来，艾伦和妻子艾琳决定去参加一个动漫展。

00:05:16.590 --> 00:05:31.980
<v Allen>Because, obviously, I'm kind of a nerd and I enjoy that sort of thing. But I went there, and I wasn't really having a very good time. And you know, I was taking a break from walking around in the convention, and I'm sitting in a little cafe.
<v Allen>因为显而易见，我有点宅，喜欢那种东西。但去了之后，我感觉并不好。你知道，我在会场溜达了一圈后休息，坐在一个小咖啡馆里。

00:05:31.980 --> 00:05:52.080
<v Allen>And I'm having something to drink or eating a sandwich. I don't really remember. But there's this guy there and he says, hey, I like your beard. And I want, oh, um, thanks. And he gave me this little card, and what it is, it's a recruitment card for this Fraternal Order of Real-Bearded Santas.
<v Allen>我在喝点东西或者是吃三明治，记不太清了。有个家伙走过来对我说：“嘿，我喜欢你的胡子。”我说：“哦，嗯，谢谢。”然后他给了我这张小卡片，是“真胡子圣诞老人兄弟会”的招募卡。

00:05:52.080 --> 00:06:03.750
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>Huh. There's something very sort of tied to the real world about the idea of recruiting a Santa, when it feels like it should just be like, some snow falls on your shoulder or something and you become Santa.
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>哈，招募圣诞老人的想法听起来太现实了，毕竟这事儿听起来应该像是什么……比如雪花落到你的肩膀上，你就成了圣诞老人那样。

00:06:03.750 --> 00:06:14.250
<v Allen>Yeah. But no, this was just a guy sitting around at a Comic-Con.
<v Allen>确实，但这只是一个在漫展上闲逛的家伙给我的。

00:06:14.250 --> 00:06:28.280
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>I talked to over a dozen first-time Santas in reporting the story. Nearly everyone had this in common. It was someone else who first identified them as Santa. Turns out, that is the magical snow that falls on your shoulder.
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>我在采访这个故事时，采访了十多位第一次当圣诞老人的新手。几乎每个人都有这个共同点：是别人先认定他们是圣诞老人的。事实证明，那确实就是落在你肩上的那抹魔法雪花。

00:06:28.280 --> 00:06:38.540
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>Allen shows a business card to his wife, Erin, who suggests he dress up for their godson's kids. Christmas is still months away, but it might give him something to look forward to.
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>艾伦把名片给妻子艾琳看，她建议他为教子的孩子们打扮一番。虽然离圣诞节还有几个月，但这也许能给他一些期待。

00:06:38.540 --> 00:06:50.210
<v Allen>I'm thinking, OK, well, I do have this beard now. And I've always been definitely on the rotund side of things. So I've got that working for me.
<v Allen>我想，好吧，既然我现在有这胡子了。而且我一直偏胖，所以这点条件还不错。

00:06:50.210 --> 00:06:56.630
<v Allen>And I went, well, I should quit feeling sorry for myself and maybe do something with this now.
<v Allen>所以我决定，不该再顾影自怜了，或许现在该用这胡子做点什么。

00:06:56.630 --> 00:07:12.310
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>Allen figures, he'll play Santa, just this once, as a nice thing to do for his family. Since he's like a grandfather to these kids, Allen was worried they might recognize him. He bleached his beard white and asked a friend who knows how to sew to make him a red suit.
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>艾伦心想，就当这次是为家人做件好事，扮一次圣诞老人。因为他就像这些孩子的祖父一样，艾伦担心他们会认出他。他把胡子漂成了白色，还请一位会缝纫的朋友给他做了一套红色的套装。

00:07:12.310 --> 00:07:24.540
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>But on Christmas Eve, as he's heading over to the house, he realizes he actually has no idea what to do. So he just tries to imagine what a real Santa might do, then opens the door and makes an entrance.
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>但在平安夜前往那家人的路上，他意识到自己其实根本不知道该做什么。所以他试着想象真正的圣诞老人会怎么做，然后推开门登场。

00:07:24.540 --> 00:07:29.520
<v Santa Allen>Ho, ho, ho! Who builds a house without a chimney?
<v Santa Allen>吼、吼、吼！是谁没装烟囱就建了房子？

00:07:29.520 --> 00:07:31.780
<v Allen>And they're freaking out.
<v Allen>他们都惊呆了。

00:07:31.780 --> 00:07:33.550
<v Santa Allen>Is it Christmas Eve?
<v Santa Allen>今天是平安夜吗？

00:07:33.550 --> 00:07:37.270
<v Allen>They have no idea it's me. It was the furthest thing from their mind.
<v Allen>他们根本不知道是我，这完全出乎他们的意料。

00:07:37.270 --> 00:07:38.350
<v Santa Allen>Let's do this anyway!
<v Santa Allen>不管怎样，让我们开始吧！

00:07:38.350 --> 00:07:53.900
<v Allen>I didn't have tremendous expectations going into it. And then, when I did it, it was just this tremendous rush of emotions, and that's not a thing that happens to me a lot. I've been a pretty buttoned-up person most of my life.
<v Allen>去之前我并没有抱太大的期望。但当我真的做了之后，那种情绪的喷涌而出，是我生活中不常有的。我大半辈子都是个非常内敛的人。

00:07:53.900 --> 00:08:07.090
<v Allen>So when it was Santa for them, it became Santa for me. It's just one of the most powerful things I've ever felt in my life.
<v Allen>所以，当我成了他们的圣诞老人时，我也成了我自己的圣诞老人。这是我一生中感受到的最强大的事情之一。

00:08:07.090 --> 00:08:21.970
<v Allen>When I'm him, I'm a completely different person. And all my little neuroses and doubts and faults and foibles-- it's all gone. And all the problems that Allen has-- Santa doesn't have any of those.
<v Allen>当我是他时，我完全变成了另一个人。我所有的神经质、怀疑、缺点和怪癖都消失了。艾伦所有的烦恼——圣诞老人一个也没有。

00:08:21.970 --> 00:08:47.000
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>Allen loved this new version of himself. But pretty soon, Christmas was over, and so was medical leave, which meant Allen's beard had to go. Because as a corrections officer, he had to be able to wear a gas mask in case a riot broke out at the prison, and you can't properly fit a gas mask over a beard. Allen couldn't afford to quit, so he grabbed his clippers and made himself shave off his beard.
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>艾伦爱上了这个全新的自己。但很快，圣诞节结束了，病假也结束了，这意味着艾伦必须刮掉胡子。因为作为一名惩教官，他必须在监狱发生骚乱时戴上防毒面具，而防毒面具在胡子上是戴不严实的。艾伦负担不起辞职的后果，所以他抓起推子，把自己剃成了光脸。

00:08:47.000 --> 00:08:55.870
<v Allen>[SIGHS] This is going to sound really weird. I still have that beard in a Ziploc bag.
<v Allen>[叹气] 这听起来真的很奇怪，但我还把那把胡子保存在密封袋里。

00:08:55.870 --> 00:08:56.770
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>Really?
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>真的吗？

00:08:56.770 --> 00:09:00.580
<v Allen>Yeah. I mourned that beard.
<v Allen>是啊，我为那把胡子哀悼过。

00:09:00.580 --> 00:09:02.880
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>Why do you still have it?
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>你为什么还留着它？

00:09:02.880 --> 00:09:23.010
<v Allen>[SIGHS] I probably shouldn't. But it meant a lot to me. It was the key to the door that opened that let me do this. And when I had to shave that beard off, I was pretty unhappy about it. I was upset.
<v Allen>[叹气] 我可能不该留着。但它对我意义重大。它是开启我做这件事的大门的钥匙。当我不得不刮掉胡子时，我非常难过，我甚至很痛苦。

00:09:23.010 --> 00:09:34.250
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>The next year, Allen bought a fake beard to play Santa. But it looked so bad, he couldn't bring himself to wear it. So two years pass without playing Santa.
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>第二年，艾伦买了一把假胡子来扮圣诞老人。但那看起来太糟糕了，他实在没法戴出去。于是，又有两年时间没能扮成圣诞老人。

00:09:34.250 --> 00:09:45.620
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>And then, thanks in part to his wife, Erin, who was making enough money as an engineer to support them, Allen is finally able to retire. And he throws himself into becoming Santa.
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>后来，多亏了他的妻子艾琳（她作为工程师赚的钱足够养活他们），艾伦终于得以退休。他全身心地投入到成为圣诞老人的事业中。

00:09:45.620 --> 00:10:00.920
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>The goal is to become a professional Santa-- the kind that someone might hire for, say, a private party or a parade or-- the Holy Grail of Santa engagements-- a chair gig. First thing-- Allen starts growing his beard back.
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>目标是成为一名专业的圣诞老人——那种会被人雇佣去参加私人派对、游行，或者圣诞老人界圣杯——“坐镇”工作的人。第一件事，艾伦开始重新留胡子。

00:10:00.920 --> 00:10:18.200
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>Then, he signs up for Santa's school, a weekend intensive where you learn everything, from what to say to skeptical kids to how to make an entrance. He meets lots of other Santas, who offer helpful tips like how to sneak in a bathroom break while staying in character. Apparently, you're supposed to say you have to make a call to the North Pole.
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>接着，他报名参加了圣诞老人学校，那是一个周末强化班，你可以学到一切，从怎么应付质疑的孩子到如何登场。他结识了许多其他圣诞老人，他们提供了一些实用的窍门，比如如何在保持角色形象的情况下溜去洗手间。显然，你应该说你要给北极打个电话。

00:10:18.200 --> 00:10:41.930
<v Allen>Finding out there's this entire world you didn't know about has been a little weird. And sometimes you wonder how you got to this place in life. This is a deeply weird thing for middle-aged and elderly guys to do. But-- but it's kind of wonderful. I mean, it's-- but it's deeply strange at the same time.
<v Allen>发现居然有这样一个我以前从未听说过的世界，感觉真有点奇怪。有时你会纳闷自己是怎么走到这一步的。对于中老年男人来说，这真是件非常古怪的事，但同时，又有一种奇妙的魅力。

00:10:41.930 --> 00:10:58.760
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>By the time I'm talking to him, Allen's spent almost a full year preparing for the role. He's a diligent and serious student of the Santa tradition, but there's one part of it he doesn't believe in-- the naughty list. Surprising, maybe, given his last job.
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>当我采访他时，艾伦已经为这个角色准备了近一年。他是圣诞老人传统中勤奋而认真的学生，但有一点他不相信——“淘气名单”。考虑到他上一份工作，这可能有点让人惊讶。

00:10:58.760 --> 00:11:03.530
<v Allen>You've been bad, you're on the naughty list. That's no way to be.
<v Allen>“你表现不好，所以你在淘气名单上。”这种方式是不对的。

00:11:03.530 --> 00:11:04.880
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>Feels punitive.
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>感觉像是在惩罚。

00:11:04.880 --> 00:11:19.180
<v Allen>Exactly. And that's-- I've seen punitive, and I don't think it works. So I want to be able to lift them up, rather than put them in a corner somewhere and tell them that you're this thing.
<v Allen>没错。我见过惩罚，我不认为它有效。所以我希望能激励他们，而不是把他们关在某个角落，告诉他们“你是那样的人”。

00:11:19.180 --> 00:11:24.610
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>Being Santa lets them work on the other side of the ledger, which feels a lot better to him.
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>成为圣诞老人让他能够在那本账簿的另一边工作，这让他感觉好多了。

00:11:24.610 --> 00:11:49.240
<v Allen>I tried to do that job as well as I could, but you know, it was really difficult. And when you run into a lot of people that are sort of on the naughty list, and you can't do anything for them, and they can't do anything for themselves, it feels kind of hopeless. That's a lesson I learned from my career. People need redemption.
<v Allen>我以前尽我所能做好那份工作，但这真的很难。当你遇到很多处于“淘气名单”上的人，而你却无能为力，他们自己也无法自救时，那种感觉真的很绝望。这是我从职业生涯中获得的教训：人们需要救赎。

00:11:49.240 --> 00:12:19.480
<v Allen>They need to be accepted back into the fold, even if they've made mistakes. You know, finding a way to forgive other people sort of gives you an opening to forgive yourself. And there's a box of Kleenex here. [BLOWS NOSE]
<v Allen>他们需要被重新接纳，即使他们犯过错。你知道，找到原谅别人的方法，也为你原谅自己打开了一扇窗。这里有盒纸巾。[擤鼻涕]

00:12:19.480 --> 00:12:29.920
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>The day has finally arrived for Allen's first-ever chair gig. We're driving to the event, which is at an abandoned Pier 1 in an outdoor mall, about a half an hour from his house.
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>艾伦第一次“坐镇”工作的日子终于到了。我们开车前往目的地，那是一个离他家半小时车程、位于室外购物中心里的一家废弃的 Pier 1 门店。

00:12:29.920 --> 00:12:48.610
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>We hop out of the car and meet Joanna Garcia, who hired Allen for the event. She lets us in through the back door of the building. There's a single clothing rack with T-shirts on it. Allen takes the shirts off, so he can use the rack to hang all the pieces of a Santa outfit. But he's so nervous, he keeps dropping stuff.
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>我们跳下车，见到了雇佣艾伦的乔安娜·加西亚。她带我们从建筑后门进去。里面只有一个挂 T 恤的架子。艾伦把衣服取下来，好用架子挂他的圣诞老人装束。但他太紧张了，手里的东西掉个不停。

00:12:48.610 --> 00:12:55.030
<v Allen>I'm kind of almost jittery. It almost feels like I'm doing slapstick or something. [LAUGHS]
<v Allen>我简直紧张得发抖，感觉像是在表演闹剧一样。[笑]

00:12:55.030 --> 00:13:11.650
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>A line forms outside while Allen changes into his costume. He's got a fur-lined red robe and matching hat and a green vest with gold buttons underneath. He's wearing big black boots and little half-moon spectacles that, I have to say, really pull the whole outfit together.
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>在艾伦换衣服的时候，门外已经排起了队。他穿上一件红色的毛绒长袍，戴上配套的帽子，里面还穿了一件带金纽扣的绿色背心。他穿着黑色的大靴子，戴着那种半月形小眼镜，我不得不说，这眼镜让整套打扮瞬间立住了。

00:13:11.650 --> 00:13:18.860
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>But he still just looks like a guy in a costume. And then the door is open, and the kids rush in.
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>但他看起来依然只是个穿着戏服的人。然后门开了，孩子们涌了进来。

00:13:18.860 --> 00:13:19.720
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>[BELLS RINGING]
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>[铃铛响]

00:13:19.720 --> 00:13:25.330
<v Santa Allen>Hello, everyone! Merry Christmas! Ho, ho! I love your dress!
<v Santa Allen>大家好！圣诞快乐！吼、吼！我喜欢你的裙子！

00:13:25.330 --> 00:13:48.160
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>Suddenly, he's Santa. Confident, arms stretched wide, beaming-- whatever the opposite of neurotic is, he is that. Two little girls walk in and spot him, and their eyes seem to double in size, like they're seeing something truly magical. It's clear they have no idea this is his first time doing this. To them, he's Santa.
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>突然间，他成了圣诞老人。自信、双臂张开、笑容满面——不管神经质的反义词是什么，他现在就是那样。两个小女孩走进来看到他，眼睛似乎瞬间瞪大了一倍，仿佛她们真的看到了奇迹。很明显，她们完全不知道这是他第一次做这件事。对她们来说，他就是圣诞老人。

00:13:48.160 --> 00:13:50.470
<v Santa Allen>OK! Are we ready, everybody?
<v Santa Allen>好了！大家都准备好了吗？

00:13:50.470 --> 00:13:50.980
<v Child>Yeah.
<v Child>准备好了！

00:13:50.980 --> 00:13:52.350
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>The day begins.
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>这一天开始了。

00:13:52.350 --> 00:13:55.420
<v Santa Allen>Do you know what you want for Christmas, sweetheart?
<v Santa Allen>宝贝，你知道你想要什么圣诞礼物吗？

00:13:55.420 --> 00:13:56.560
<v Child>Monster trucks.
<v Child>怪兽卡车。

00:13:56.560 --> 00:14:00.820
<v Santa Allen>Monster trucks? I love monster trucks! [ROARS]
<v Santa Allen>怪兽卡车？我最喜欢怪兽卡车了！[咆哮]

00:14:00.820 --> 00:14:14.080
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>As you can hear, he's got his kid voice on. And I don't know what I expected, exactly, but this job requires just so much pep. Santa Allen is tap-dancing as fast as he can--
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>如你所听到的，他用上了“孩子腔”。我不知道自己具体期待什么，但这份工作确实需要极大的热情。圣诞老人艾伦正竭尽全力地卖力表演——

00:14:14.080 --> 00:14:18.190
<v Santa Allen>(SINGING) Jingle bells, jingle bells, jingle all the way.
<v Santa Allen>(唱) 铃儿响叮当，铃儿响叮当，一路叮当响。

00:14:18.190 --> 00:14:20.740
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>--to keep the kids happy and engaged--
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>为了让孩子们保持快乐和参与度——

00:14:20.740 --> 00:14:24.550
<v Santa Allen>Fist bump! Yeah! Blow it up! Boom!
<v Santa Allen>碰拳！耶！加油！嘣！

00:14:24.550 --> 00:14:27.840
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>--so the photographer, Sam, can snap a good picture for the parents.
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>这样摄影师萨姆才能为家长们拍出一张好照片。

00:14:27.840 --> 00:14:32.740
<v Santa Allen>(SINGING) Paw Patrol, Paw Patrol, paw, paw, paw, paw, paw, paw, paw, Paw Patrol!
<v Santa Allen>(唱) 汪汪队，汪汪队，汪、汪、汪、汪、汪、汪、汪，汪汪队！

00:14:32.740 --> 00:14:50.950
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>Watching Santa Allen, I thought about this thing another Santa-- a pastor named Santa Don-- told me. Santa Don said that back when he was in graduate school for psychology, on his first day of class, his professor asked, how many of you have people who are already coming to you for counseling?
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>看着圣诞老人艾伦，我想到另一位圣诞老人——一位名叫圣诞老人唐的牧师告诉我的话。圣诞老人唐说，当年他在读心理学研究生时，开学第一天，教授问大家：“你们中有多少人已经有人来找你们寻求咨询了？”

00:14:50.950 --> 00:15:03.100
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>Don and a few other students raise their hands. Then the professor goes, I'm going to be honest with you. If people aren't already coming to you, getting a degree from here probably won't change that.
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>唐和其他几名学生举起了手。教授然后说：“我跟你们说实话吧。如果现在还没人找你们，单凭拿个学位可能也改变不了什么。”

00:15:03.100 --> 00:15:18.410
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>You're either the kind of person people want to confide in, or you're not. Don thinks that the same is true for Santa. There's some innate Santa essence, some Santa vibe, that you either have or you don't.
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>你要么是那种让人想倾诉的人，要么就不是。唐认为圣诞老人也是这样。有一种天生的圣诞老人本质，某种“圣诞气氛”，要么你有，要么你没有。

00:15:18.410 --> 00:15:26.170
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>And here, on his first chair gig, Santa Allen seemed to have it, though it didn't always go so smoothly with every kid.
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>在这里，在他的第一次“坐镇”工作中，圣诞老人艾伦似乎拥有这种特质，尽管并不是对每个孩子都那么顺利。

00:15:26.170 --> 00:15:26.500
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>[CHILD CRYING]
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>[孩子在哭]

00:15:26.500 --> 00:15:31.000
<v Santa Allen>(SINGING) Here comes Santa Claus, here comes Santa Claus, right down Santa Claus--
<v Santa Allen>(唱) 圣诞老人来啦，圣诞老人来啦，圣诞老人正朝这里——

00:15:31.000 --> 00:15:32.920
<v Santa Allen>[CHILD CONTINUES CRYING]
<v Santa Allen>[孩子继续在哭]

00:15:32.920 --> 00:15:36.760
<v Santa Allen>Oh! [LAUGHS]
<v Santa Allen>哦！[笑]

00:15:36.760 --> 00:15:38.740
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>There were a lot of crying babies.
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>哭闹的婴儿还真不少。

00:15:38.740 --> 00:15:41.590
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>[BABY CRYING]
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>[婴儿在哭]

00:15:41.590 --> 00:15:48.700
<v Santa Allen>Oh! I'm so sorry! We'll get you next year! I'll see you on Christmas Eve, my friend.
<v Santa Allen>哦！真抱歉！我们明年再见！我的朋友，平安夜我会再见到你的。

00:15:48.700 --> 00:15:57.400
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>Sometimes kids asked Santa Allen for things that threw him for a loop, like the boy who said he wanted trouble for Christmas, or the girl who just wanted socks.
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>有时孩子们会问一些让圣诞老人艾伦不知所措的问题，比如有个男孩说他想要“麻烦”作为圣诞礼物，还有个女孩只想要袜子。

00:15:57.400 --> 00:16:03.460
<v Santa Allen>But, like, character socks, right? Good socks? No? Just regular sock socks? OK, that's good.
<v Santa Allen>但是，是那种卡通图案的袜子，对吧？好的那种？不？普通的袜子？好吧，那也不错。

00:16:03.460 --> 00:16:27.790
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>One little girl wanted two hamsters, but as she asked for them, her parents stood behind her with wide eyes, vigorously shaking their heads, no. Of course, there were some awkward moments, like when Santa Allen accidentally mistaked a boy with long hair for a girl. And this kid, who looked like a tiny adult, and who is not having it with Santa Allen's questions--
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>一个小女孩想要两只仓鼠，但她问的时候，她父母站在身后瞪大眼睛，拼命摇头说不。当然，也有尴尬时刻，比如圣诞老人艾伦不小心把一个长头发的男孩认成了女孩。还有这个孩子，看起来像个小大人，根本不买圣诞老人问题的账——

00:16:27.790 --> 00:16:31.480
<v Santa Allen>I have animals. Do you know what kind of animals I have?
<v Santa Allen>我有小动物。你知道我养了什么动物吗？

00:16:31.480 --> 00:16:33.520
<v Child>Uh, the pretend kind.
<v Child>呃，那种假装的。

00:16:33.520 --> 00:16:37.300
<v Santa Allen>No, the real reindeer. They have antlers like this.
<v Santa Allen>不，是真正的驯鹿。它们有长成这样的鹿角。

00:16:37.300 --> 00:16:40.270
<v Child>Yeah, I know. I've seen them on TV.
<v Child>是啊，我知道。我在电视上见过。

00:16:40.270 --> 00:16:43.300
<v Santa Allen>Have you seen Rudolph? Have you seen his nose?
<v Santa Allen>你见过鲁道夫吗？你见过他的鼻子吗？

00:16:43.300 --> 00:16:46.340
<v Child>Yeah, I know. His nose lights up.
<v Child>见过，我知道。他的鼻子会发光。

00:16:46.340 --> 00:16:52.420
<v Santa Allen>Yeah it lights up, and it's red. Are you going to school right now?
<v Santa Allen>是的，它会发光，而且是红色的。你现在在上学吗？

00:16:52.420 --> 00:16:53.110
<v Child>No.
<v Child>没有。

00:16:53.110 --> 00:16:53.770
<v Santa Allen>No?
<v Santa Allen>没有？

00:16:53.770 --> 00:16:55.510
<v Child>I'm barely four.
<v Child>我才刚四岁。

00:16:55.510 --> 00:16:58.180
<v Santa Allen>Oh, some people go to school when they're four.
<v Santa Allen>哦，有些人四岁就开始上学了。

00:16:58.180 --> 00:17:17.680
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>Santa Allen had imagined fielding all these big, hard questions from kids. But in practice, the real work of imitating Santa is more like working in a factory line, a quick back-and-forth, snap a picture, then onto the next. The hardest question he ended up getting was about Mrs. Claus.
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>圣诞老人艾伦曾想象过自己要应付孩子们各种各样的难题。但实际上，模仿圣诞老人的真正工作更像是工厂流水线，快速的一问一答，拍张照，然后换下一个。他最后遇到的最难的问题是关于圣诞老奶奶的。

00:17:17.680 --> 00:17:19.720
<v Child>I was going to ask you--
<v Child>我正想问你——

00:17:19.720 --> 00:17:20.380
<v Santa Allen>OK.
<v Santa Allen>好。

00:17:20.380 --> 00:17:30.520
<v Child>--if you can ask Mrs. Claus if she actually does bake cookies. So like, if she does, can she send me one?
<v Child>——如果你能问问圣诞老奶奶，她真的会烤饼干吗？如果她真的烤，能送我一个吗？

00:17:30.520 --> 00:17:41.620
<v Santa Allen>I don't-- she doesn't-- she just mostly makes them for the North Pole, but we'll ask. And she is important. She is the CEO of the North Pole. Mrs. Claus is actually my boss.
<v Santa Allen>我不……她不太……她主要是在北极烤，但我们会问问的。而且她很重要，她是北极的 CEO。圣诞老奶奶其实是我的老板。

00:17:41.620 --> 00:18:08.950
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>Allen wanted to yes-and the little girl, but didn't want to promise cookies and set her up to be disappointed on Christmas Day. So instead, he pivoted to a feminist message. He later told me, he wants to update the image of Mrs. Claus for kids, especially for girls. He doesn't like the idea that she just bakes cookies and putters around the North Pole all day.
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>艾伦想给那个小女孩一个肯定的答复，但又不想承诺饼干结果让她在圣诞节失望。于是他转而传达了一个女权主义的信息。他后来告诉我，他想为孩子们更新圣诞老奶奶的形象，尤其是对女孩们。他不希望她只是整天在北极烤饼干和闲逛。

00:18:08.950 --> 00:18:26.890
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>At 1:00 PM, the doors are closed. Santa Allen's been there for two hours, talked to over 100 kids. He changes out of his costume and into what's referred to as "Santa casual" -- red clothing, but not the whole shebang. Christmas Lite. He packs up, and we get in the car.
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>下午 1 点，大门关上了。圣诞老人艾伦在那里待了两个小时，与 100 多个孩子聊了天。他换下了戏服，穿上了所谓的“圣诞便装”——红色的衣服，但不是全套的那种，也就是“轻量级圣诞节”。他收拾好东西，我们上了车。

00:18:26.890 --> 00:18:45.030
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>We're about to drive back to his house, when Allen realizes he's left his backpack inside the store. This is a problem for him, because he really doesn't want to go back and get it and have people see him when he's not in the full Santa costume. But as we're walking back to the Pier 1, a guy in a car notices Allen.
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>我们正要开车回家时，艾伦意识到他把背包落在店里了。这对他来说是个问题，因为他真不想回去取包，不想让人在他没穿全套戏服时看到他。但当我们走回 Pier 1 时，一个开车的人注意到了艾伦。

00:18:45.030 --> 00:18:46.480
<v Man>Santa?
<v Man>圣诞老人？

00:18:46.480 --> 00:18:51.050
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>Allen puts his finger up to his lips, as if to say, shh!
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>艾伦把手指放在嘴唇上，好像在说：嘘！

00:18:51.050 --> 00:18:51.450
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>[ALLEN LAUGHS]
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>[艾伦笑]

00:18:51.450 --> 00:18:52.330
<v Man>I won't say nothing.
<v Man>我什么都不会说的。

00:18:52.330 --> 00:18:53.950
<v Allen>(LAUGHING) OK.
<v Allen>[笑] 好的。

00:18:53.950 --> 00:19:05.260
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>I won't say nothing, the guy says. Then, we're standing in front of the store, waiting to be let back in, when a girl in the backseat of a car driving by yells, Santa!
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>“我什么都不会说的，”那个人说。接着，我们站在店门前等着被放进去，这时一辆经过的车后座里的女孩尖叫道：“圣诞老人！”

00:19:05.260 --> 00:19:09.000
<v Allen>Oh, jeez. I kind of don't want to ruin the image for anybody.
<v Allen>哦，天哪。我有点不想破坏在任何人眼里的形象。

00:19:09.000 --> 00:19:10.460
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>You feel embarrassed.
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>你感到尴尬了。

00:19:10.460 --> 00:19:11.950
<v Man>I-- yeah.
<v Man>我……是的。

00:19:11.950 --> 00:19:23.050
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>Allen tries to tuck himself into the corner of the building by the door to hide from people, but it doesn't work. At all. Everyone who passes by double-takes him or yells at him.
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>艾伦试着把自己缩在店门口的角落里想躲开人们，但完全没用。每一个经过的人都会回头看他，或者对他大喊。

00:19:23.050 --> 00:19:23.920
<v Woman>Hey, Santa!
<v Woman>嘿，圣诞老人！

00:19:23.920 --> 00:19:25.390
<v Allen>Hi.
<v Allen>嗨。

00:19:25.390 --> 00:19:27.220
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>Allen looks miserable.
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>艾伦看起来很痛苦。

00:19:27.220 --> 00:19:29.800
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>It's funny, you've transformed back into Allen.
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>这很有意思，你又变回了艾伦。

00:19:29.800 --> 00:19:30.340
<v Allen>I know.
<v Allen>我知道。

00:19:30.340 --> 00:19:31.210
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>Without the robe.
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>没有长袍。

00:19:31.210 --> 00:19:35.170
<v Allen>I know. You love watching me squirm like this.
<v Allen>我知道。你很喜欢看我这样局促不安。

00:19:35.170 --> 00:19:39.260
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>No! I don't. But I'm struck by the transformation.
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>不！我没有。但我被这种转变震惊了。

00:19:39.260 --> 00:19:39.970
<v Allen>Yeah.
<v Allen>是啊。

00:19:39.970 --> 00:19:41.560
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>How different you are.
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>你完全变了一个人。

00:19:41.560 --> 00:19:45.430
<v Allen>I guess I'm back to being Clark Kent or something. I don't know.
<v Allen>我猜我又变回了克拉克·肯特之类的吧，我不知道。

00:19:45.430 --> 00:19:51.200
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>You're literally cowering in a corner. This is not the man I saw 30 minutes ago.
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>你刚才简直是缩在角落里。这跟我半小时前见到的那个人完全不一样。

00:19:51.200 --> 00:19:56.200
<v Allen>I'm-- I'm not him. I'm me.
<v Allen>我……我不是他，我就是我。

00:19:56.200 --> 00:20:25.620
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>Allen did not plan on ending his first chair gig glued to the side of a Pier 1. But there is a part of him, he tells me later, that's relieved to be himself again. All that cheeriness, being so enthusiastically on-- it's exhausting being Santa.
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>艾伦没计划让他的第一份坐镇工作以缩在 Pier 门店边上结束。但他告诉我，他内心深处其实挺庆幸又能做回自己了。那种欢快、那种热情地保持表演状态——当圣诞老人真的很耗体力。

00:20:25.620 --> 00:20:33.030
<v Ira Glass>Aviva DeKornfeld is one of the producers of our show.
<v Ira Glass>阿维瓦·德科恩菲尔德是我们节目的制作人之一。

00:20:33.030 --> 00:20:49.030
<v Ira Glass>Act Two, Jews for Jesus, for Just One Day. For Alex Edelman, it's easy to remember his first Christmas, because it was his only Christmas. He was raised as an Orthodox Jew. And he says his family was very Jewish, casually dropped Hebrew around the house.
<v Ira Glass>第二幕，为耶稣而作的犹太人，只做一天。对于亚历克斯·埃德尔曼来说，记住他的第一个圣诞节很容易，因为那是他唯一的一个圣诞节。他在正统犹太教家庭中长大。他说他的家庭非常传统，家里经常会蹦出希伯来语。

00:20:49.030 --> 00:21:01.030
<v Ira Glass>He and his brother, AJ, called their parents Eema and Abba for Mom and Dad. He's a comedian and tells this story about how they were so Jewish and so surrounded by Jews, he didn't even know he was Jewish for a while.
<v Ira Glass>他和哥哥 AJ 叫父母为“Eema”和“Abba”，也就是妈妈和爸爸。他是个喜剧演员，经常讲起他们家如何沉浸在犹太氛围中，以至于他有一段时间甚至不知道自己是犹太人。

