00:31:14.240 --> 00:31:26.810
<v Alex Edelman>And the teacher's like, you didn't have Christmas. You had Hanukkah, three weeks ago or nine months from now, depending on the year. Anyway, non-Jews believe in this guy, called Santa Claus.
<v Alex Edelman>“老师说：‘你们没过圣诞节。你们过了光明节，那是三周前或者九个月后，视年份而定。总之，非犹太人相信有个叫圣诞老人的人。’”

00:31:26.810 --> 00:31:40.490
<v Alex Edelman>And in my mind's eye, I remember AJ raising his hand and saying, we had Santa Claus. That's what I remember. But there are conflicting accounts here.
<v Alex Edelman>而在我的记忆里，我记得 AJ 举手说：“我们过了圣诞节。”这是我记忆中的版本。但这里有相互冲突的说法。

00:31:40.490 --> 00:32:04.280
<v Alex Edelman>Apparently, AJ didn't raise his hand. Apparently, AJ, in the words of the teacher, lifted two fingers, like he was ordering a second Martini. And AJ didn't say, we had Santa Claus. Apparently, AJ just went, we know Santa Claus.
<v Alex Edelman>显然 AJ 没有举手。显然，在老师的描述中，AJ 竖起了两根手指，就像是在点第二杯马提尼。而且 AJ 没说“我们过了圣诞节”。显然 AJ 说的是：“我们认识圣诞老人。”

00:32:04.280 --> 00:32:07.350
<v Alex Edelman>[AUDIENCE LAUGHTER]
<v Alex Edelman>[观众笑声]

00:32:07.350 --> 00:32:25.500
<v Alex Edelman>And the teacher's like, you don't know Santa Claus, AJ! And AJ's like, mmm, pretty sure I know Santa Claus. And the other kids are like, who's Santa Claus? AJ's like, oh! He's amazing! He's a fat man, and he comes down non-Jewish chimneys in the middle of the night, and he gives gifts to the young gentiles.
<v Alex Edelman>老师说：“你不认识圣诞老人，AJ！”AJ 说：“嗯，我很确定我认识圣诞老人。”其他孩子问：“谁是圣诞老人？”AJ 说：“噢！他太棒了！他是个胖子，深夜从非犹太人的烟囱里钻进来，把礼物送给年轻的非犹太人。”

00:32:25.500 --> 00:32:32.250
<v Alex Edelman>He's like Elijah, but instead of wine, there's cookies and gifts. But last night, because he's friends with our dad--
<v Alex Edelman>“他就像以利亚，但不是喝酒，而是吃饼干和送礼物。但昨晚，因为他是我爸的朋友——”

00:32:32.250 --> 00:32:39.340
<v Alex Edelman>[AUDIENCE LAUGHTER]
<v Alex Edelman>[观众笑声]

00:32:39.340 --> 00:33:07.420
<v Alex Edelman>--he came down our chimney, and he left this Walkman underneath the tree that my parents put in the garage, and he ate all of the cookies! The principal said, Professor Edelman, is this true? And apparently, my father just went, no. It's not entirely true.
<v Alex Edelman>“——他从我们烟囱钻下来，把这台随身听留在车库里的圣诞树下，还把所有的饼干都吃了！”校长问：“埃德尔曼教授，这是真的吗？”显然我父亲回答：“不，不完全是真的。”

00:33:07.420 --> 00:33:14.720
<v Alex Edelman>And the principal said, what do you mean it's not entirely true? And my father said, Cheryl and I ate the cookies.
<v Alex Edelman>校长问：“什么叫不完全是真的？”我父亲说：“谢丽尔和我吃了饼干。”

00:33:14.720 --> 00:33:22.140
<v Alex Edelman>[AUDIENCE LAUGHTER]
<v Alex Edelman>[观众笑声]

00:33:22.140 --> 00:33:33.320
<v Alex Edelman>AJ's wrong. Santa didn't come to our house. And my mom says there was a significant pause on the other end of the phone. And then, the principal went, I know Santa didn't come to your house!
<v Alex Edelman>“AJ 错了。圣诞老人没来我们家。”我妈说电话那头沉默了许久。然后校长说：“我知道圣诞老人没来你们家！”

00:33:33.320 --> 00:33:39.160
<v Alex Edelman>[AUDIENCE LAUGHTER]
<v Alex Edelman>[观众笑声]

00:33:39.160 --> 00:34:02.140
<v Alex Edelman>Yet, Christmas? My father explained. He explained that Kelly was bereft and that she had nowhere to go, and that he had reservations about this. But he let it happen, because he thought it was actually a really valuable, teachable moment, where you could explain to his children that doing the thing centered in Jewish values may not always appear conventionally Jewish.
<v Alex Edelman>那圣诞节呢？我父亲解释说，凯莉当时非常无助，无处可去，他虽然有所顾虑，但还是让它发生了。因为他觉得这是一个非常有价值的教育时刻，可以教孩子理解：做符合犹太价值观的事，并不一定总是看起来像传统的犹太行为。

00:34:02.140 --> 00:34:14.290
<v Alex Edelman>And more than anything else, doing this for this woman, who really had nowhere to go, really-- it was a classic example of what Jews would traditionally call mitzvah, which means good deed. Principal listens, Dad explains. My dad finishes.
<v Alex Edelman>而且最重要的是，为这个无处可去的女人做这件事，是犹太传统中“善行”（Mitzvah）的经典例子。校长听着，我爸解释着，我爸讲完了。

00:34:14.290 --> 00:34:37.690
<v Alex Edelman>The principal said, Professor Edelman, I understand where you're coming from, but you're wrong. Because what you've actually done is you've introduced this holiday that is foreign and corrosive into your home. And you've clearly confused your children, and perhaps harmed them permanently.
<v Alex Edelman>校长说：“埃德尔曼教授，我理解你的初衷，但你错了。因为你实际上做的是把这个外来的、腐蚀性的节日引入了你的家。你显然搞混了你的孩子，也许还造成了永久性的伤害。”

00:34:37.690 --> 00:34:49.270
<v Alex Edelman>Well, you've given them this holiday, and next year, you're going to take it away from them. So what's that going to do? This isn't a good deed. This isn't a mitzvah. It's idol worship, which is the most grievous sin that a Jew can possibly commit.
<v Alex Edelman>“你给了他们这个节日，明年你又得把它夺走，那会怎么样？这不是善行。这是偶像崇拜，这是犹太人可能犯下的最严重的罪行。”

00:34:49.270 --> 00:35:05.440
<v Alex Edelman>And you and your wife need to begin repenting for that right now. And my father, to his credit, went-- well, clearly, rabbi, you don't understand the meaning of Christmas. And then, he hung up the phone.
<v Alex Edelman>“你和你妻子现在就需要开始忏悔。”我父亲回敬道：“好吧，看来校长你不懂圣诞节的意义。”然后他就挂断了电话。

00:35:05.440 --> 00:35:11.330
<v Alex Edelman>[AUDIENCE APPLAUSE]
<v Alex Edelman>[观众掌声]

00:35:11.330 --> 00:35:28.610
<v Ira Glass>Alex Edelman, with an excerpt of his one-man show, Just For Us, has taken the show to London in January. To find out when he's bringing the show to your city, go to AlexEdelmancomedy.com. The radio excerpt of his story was produced for our show by Aviva DeKornfeld.
<v Ira Glass>亚历克斯·埃德尔曼，单人秀《Just For Us》的节选。该剧已于一月在伦敦上演。要查找他何时来到你的城市，请访问 AlexEdelmancomedy.com。他的这个电台故事节选由阿维瓦·德科恩菲尔德为我们制作。

00:35:28.610 --> 00:35:44.970
<v Ira Glass>Coming up-- the hottest, sexiest parts of It's a Wonderful Life, and other revelations about a very old film from a first-time viewer. That's in a minute, from Chicago Public Radio, when our program continues.
<v Ira Glass>接下来——《风云人物》（It's a Wonderful Life）中最火辣、最性感的部分，以及一位初次观看者的其他发现。稍后，芝加哥公共电台节目继续。

00:35:44.970 --> 00:35:59.250
<v Ira Glass>It's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. Today's program-- Baby's First Christmas. Stories of people trying something for the very first time at Christmas, and what that does for them. We've arrived at Act Three of our show, Act Three, Buffalo Gals, Can't You Come Out Tonight?
<v Ira Glass>这里是《美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。今天的节目——《宝宝的第一个圣诞节》。故事关于人们在圣诞节第一次尝试某事，以及这给他们带来的影响。我们来到节目的第三幕，《水牛城的姑娘，你今晚不出来跳舞吗？》。

00:35:59.250 --> 00:36:18.400
<v Ira Glass>So in this story, the new experience that somebody is having at Christmas is simple. We have a grown man, a man who loves films-- classic films, obscure films, new films, all kinds of films-- and he's seen tons of them-- who, somehow, has never seen the most pervasive movie that's out there at Christmas-- It's a Wonderful Life.
<v Ira Glass>在这个故事中，某人圣诞节的新体验很简单。有一位成年男子，他热爱电影——经典片、小众片、新片，什么都看——他看过了无数电影，却不知为何从未看过圣诞期间最普及的那部影片——《风云人物》。

00:36:18.400 --> 00:36:32.740
<v Ira Glass>That man-- somebody who's been on the show a bunch, one of our former producers, Neil Drumming. Our executive editor, Emanuele Berry, is, in real life, friends with Neil, and took it upon herself to change his life forever, or possibly not at all, and show him the film.
<v Ira Glass>那个人——我们节目的常客，我们的前制作人尼尔·德拉明（Neil Drumming）。我们的执行编辑埃曼努埃尔·贝里（Emanuele Berry）在现实生活中是尼尔的朋友，她自告奋勇要改变他的人生，或者可能什么也不会改变，并带他看了这部电影。

00:36:32.740 --> 00:36:42.940
<v Emanuele Berry>I've watched a lot of movies with Neil. It's fun, because he gets so giddy and excited about showing you a movie you haven't seen. You always know you're going to have a thoughtful conversation afterwards.
<v Emanuele Berry>我和尼尔看过很多电影。这很有趣，因为他会兴高采烈地向你展示他没看过的电影。事后你们总能进行一场深刻的交流。

00:36:42.940 --> 00:36:55.970
<v Emanuele Berry>But I also sometimes hate it-- like when he recites all the dialogue in Dazed and Confused while you're trying to watch it. So how'd he miss this movie that's been on television every year since he was born?
<v Emanuele Berry>但我有时也讨厌这一点——比如当你试图看《年少轻狂》（Dazed and Confused）时，他却在旁边背诵所有的台词。那么，他是怎么错过这部从他出生起每年都在电视上播放的电影的呢？

00:36:55.970 --> 00:37:12.310
<v Neil Drumming>I really-- I feel like the Christmas movies that I love are the movies with Christmas in the background, like all British movies. But, like, Love Actually, or Die Hard, or like-- movies with Christmas in that background, Christmas in the foreground-- like, I've never seen the one with the little kid with the glasses?
<v Neil Drumming>我真的……我觉得我喜欢的圣诞电影是那种背景里有圣诞节的，比如所有的英国电影。比如《真爱至上》（Love Actually），或者是《虎胆龙威》（Die Hard）。但我从来没看过那个戴眼镜的小男孩演的那部……

00:37:12.310 --> 00:37:13.180
<v Emanuele Berry>A Christmas Story?
<v Emanuele Berry>《圣诞故事》（A Christmas Story）？

00:37:13.180 --> 00:37:29.140
<v Neil Drumming>Yeah. But, like, the movies that are just Christmas-- I just didn't have an inclination to see them. I think it's because Christmas, for me, was always in the background. Like, I was a Jehovah's Witness, so Christmas was like a thing that happened around me.
<v Neil Drumming>对。但那种纯圣诞节的电影，我就是没兴趣。我觉得是因为对我来说，圣诞节一直是在背景里的。因为我是耶和华见证人，所以圣诞节就像是发生在我身边的一件事。

00:37:29.140 --> 00:37:34.640
<v Emanuele Berry>No Christmas tree, no Christmas carols, no Christmas movies. Given that--
<v Emanuele Berry>没有圣诞树，没有圣诞颂歌，没有圣诞电影。鉴于此——

00:37:34.640 --> 00:37:39.790
<v Emanuele Berry>what is your favorite Christmas movie-- Christmas-in-the-background movie, I guess, is what we're calling them.
<v Emanuele Berry>我想我们大概可以称之为“背景圣诞节电影”，你最喜欢哪一部？

00:37:39.790 --> 00:37:46.030
<v Neil Drumming>I think Die Hard is clearly my favorite Christmas movie. I mean, I hate to be so traditional, like, as a male--
<v Neil Drumming>我想《虎胆龙威》显然是我最喜欢的圣诞电影。我是说，作为一个男人，我讨厌这么传统……

00:37:46.030 --> 00:37:46.900
<v Emanuele Berry>(LAUGHING) So traditional.
<v Emanuele Berry>[笑] 这么传统。

00:37:46.900 --> 00:37:51.850
<v Neil Drumming>I mean, because a lot of men-- a lot of people-- a lot of men will say that Die Hard is their favorite Christmas movie. It is--
<v Neil Drumming>因为很多男人——很多人都会说《虎胆龙威》是他们最喜欢的圣诞电影。它是——

00:37:51.850 --> 00:37:53.530
<v Emanuele Berry>Is it just your favorite movie?
<v Emanuele Berry>那这是你最喜欢的电影吗？

00:37:53.530 --> 00:37:56.140
<v Neil Drumming>It is in my top 5, consistently.
<v Neil Drumming>它一直都在我的前五名之内。

00:37:56.140 --> 00:38:14.500
<v Emanuele Berry>Neil, you see, is a classic example of the problem. I think there's a world of people out there who haven't seen It's a Wonderful Life, because their impression is it's just some corny Hallmark-y movie. Weirdly, I think the most popular Christmas movie out there is also somehow the most underrated by people like Neil.
<v Emanuele Berry>你看，尼尔就是这个问题的典型例子。我觉得世界上有很多人没看过《风云人物》，因为他们印象里觉得那是一部庸俗的“霍尔马克风格”的电影。奇怪的是，我认为最受欢迎的圣诞电影，在尼尔这样的人眼里，却是被低估最严重的。

00:38:14.500 --> 00:38:28.570
<v Emanuele Berry>To be upfront about this-- I love It's a Wonderful Life. I love the romance of it, the darkness, the humor. I love Jimmy Stewart, the lead actor, and his bumbling charm. Zuzu's petals, the broken banister-- I love it all.
<v Emanuele Berry>坦白说——我爱《风云人物》。我爱它的浪漫、黑暗和幽默。我爱主演吉米·斯图尔特，爱他笨拙的魅力。祖祖的花瓣，那道断了的扶手——我全爱。

00:38:28.570 --> 00:38:34.630
<v Emanuele Berry>It's not just my favorite Christmas film. It's my favorite film. And I want Neil to see what I see.
<v Emanuele Berry>它不仅是我最喜欢的圣诞电影，也是我最喜欢的电影。我想让尼尔看到我所看到的。

00:38:34.630 --> 00:38:37.150
<v Emanuele Berry>What do you know about It's a Wonderful Life?
<v Emanuele Berry>关于《风云人物》，你知道些什么？

00:38:37.150 --> 00:38:40.810
<v Neil Drumming>It's a Christmas movie, because it happened-- it's always on the air during Christmas.
<v Neil Drumming>它是一部圣诞电影，因为在圣诞节期间它总是在电视上播。

00:38:40.810 --> 00:38:41.500
<v Emanuele Berry>Mm-hmm.
<v Emanuele Berry>嗯哼。

00:38:41.500 --> 00:38:45.370
<v Neil Drumming>But-- and I know-- I think Macy's is involved or something.
<v Neil Drumming>但我知道……我想梅西百货可能参与其中什么的。

00:38:45.370 --> 00:38:54.640
<v Emanuele Berry>Wrong. Here's what else he thinks he knows. Jimmy Stewart, it's in black and white, and he saw something on Instagram that it's about a person losing everything.
<v Emanuele Berry>错了。他觉得自己还知道：吉米·斯图尔特，黑白片，他在 Instagram 上看到过关于它讲的是一个人失去了一切。

00:38:54.640 --> 00:39:00.820
<v Neil Drumming>Which makes me think it's, like, maybe some kind of, like, yuletide retelling of the Job story?
<v Neil Drumming>这让我觉得它可能是什么约伯记故事的圣诞翻拍版？

00:39:00.820 --> 00:39:01.180
<v Emanuele Berry>OK.
<v Emanuele Berry>好吧。

00:39:01.180 --> 00:39:02.830
<v Neil Drumming>I don't know. That's a guess.
<v Neil Drumming>我不知道。瞎猜的。

00:39:02.830 --> 00:39:13.570
<v Emanuele Berry>Another reason It's a Wonderful Life holds a special place in my heart-- the ending of the film actually makes me get a little teary. I'm not a crier. I'm embarrassed by the idea of a film making me cry.
<v Emanuele Berry>《风云人物》在我心中占有特殊地位的另一个原因是——电影结尾真的让我有点泪目。我不是个爱哭的人。电影让我哭这种事让我觉得难为情。

00:39:13.570 --> 00:39:17.470
<v Emanuele Berry>But for lots of people, I think It's a Wonderful Life sneaks up on you.
<v Emanuele Berry>但对许多人来说，我觉得《风云人物》总是悄无声息地触动你。

00:39:17.470 --> 00:39:18.950
<v Emanuele Berry>Do you think you will cry?
<v Emanuele Berry>你觉得你会哭吗？

00:39:18.950 --> 00:39:28.450
<v Neil Drumming>I mean, I absolutely will not cry. The only movie I can think of that I cried at was, like-- it was a Will Smith movie, where he played, like, a poor father once. I can't remember the name of that movie.
<v Neil Drumming>我是说，我绝对不会哭。我能想到的唯一让我哭的电影，是……那是威尔·史密斯演的，他演一个可怜的父亲。我不记得电影名了。

00:39:28.450 --> 00:39:29.320
<v Emanuele Berry>Pursuit of Happiness?
<v Emanuele Berry>《当幸福来敲门》（Pursuit of Happyness）？

00:39:29.320 --> 00:39:30.340
<v Neil Drumming>Yes, Pursuit of Happiness.
<v Neil Drumming>对，《当幸福来敲门》。

00:39:30.340 --> 00:39:31.450
<v Emanuele Berry>You cried during Pursuit of--
<v Emanuele Berry>你在《当幸福来敲门》时哭了？

00:39:31.450 --> 00:39:36.160
<v Neil Drumming>Because he had this thing-- he had some long speech about failure as a man, and--
<v Neil Drumming>因为他有那一段……关于作为一个男人失败的长篇大论，而且——

00:39:36.160 --> 00:39:39.580
<v Emanuele Berry>Oh, wow, I think-- I don't know, if-- you might cry, Neil.
<v Emanuele Berry>噢，哇，我想……我不知道，尼尔，你可能会哭的。

00:39:39.580 --> 00:39:50.250
<v Neil Drumming>Yeah, OK. It's-- I mean, OK, that was a few years ago. Let's see if-- if there is a long speech about failing as a man, then maybe I'll cry. Because that's a subject close to my heart.
<v Neil Drumming>是啊，好吧。我是说，那已经是几年前的事了。让我们看看如果……如果里面也有关于作为男人失败的长篇大论，也许我会哭。因为那是触动我内心深处的话题。

00:39:50.250 --> 00:39:53.510
<v Neil Drumming>[LAUGHTER]
<v Neil Drumming>[笑声]

00:39:53.510 --> 00:40:10.470
<v Emanuele Berry>We start the film. In case you, like Neil, haven't seen it, the movie is about George Bailey, a bright, goofy, and friendly young man who wants to see the world but keeps getting stuck in his hometown. First, it's a job that keeps him there. His father passes, and he takes his place running the family business.
<v Emanuele Berry>电影开始了。如果你和尼尔一样没看过，这部电影讲的是乔治·贝利，一个开朗、古怪又友善的年轻人，他想环游世界却总是被困在家乡。最初是一个工作把他留在了那里：父亲去世，他接手了家族生意。

00:40:10.470 --> 00:40:24.200
<v Emanuele Berry>Then, it's a romance. George meets Mary at a dance where they fall into a pool. And they're forced to walk home in whatever dry clothing they can wrangle together. Mary is in a bathrobe, and George is in a football uniform three sizes too big.
<v Emanuele Berry>接着是一段罗曼史。乔治在一个舞蹈派对上遇见了玛丽，他们掉进了泳池，被迫只能披着找到的干衣服走回家。玛丽裹着浴袍，乔治穿着大三个尺码的橄榄球服。

00:40:24.200 --> 00:40:31.870
<v George And Mary>(SINGING) Buffalo gal, can't you come out tonight and dance by--
<v George And Mary>(唱) 水牛城的姑娘，你今晚不出来……

00:40:31.870 --> 00:40:34.210
<v Emanuele Berry>Neil loves a romcom, a good meet-cute.
<v Emanuele Berry>尼尔喜欢浪漫喜剧，喜欢这种宿命般的相遇。

00:40:34.210 --> 00:40:39.330
<v George>You know, if it wasn't me talking, I'd say you were the prettiest girl in town.
<v George>你知道，如果不是我自己在说，我会说你是全镇最美的女孩。

00:40:39.330 --> 00:40:41.780
<v Mary>Well, why don't you say it?
<v Mary>那你为什么不说出来呢？

00:40:41.780 --> 00:40:43.290
<v George>Oh, I don't know. Maybe I will say it.
<v George>噢，我不知道。也许我会说的。

00:40:43.290 --> 00:40:46.590
<v Neil Drumming>This is hot. It's hot! This is hot!
<v Neil Drumming>这太火辣了。太火辣了！这太热了！

00:40:46.590 --> 00:40:47.400
<v Emanuele Berry>(LAUGHING) What do you mean?
<v Emanuele Berry>[笑] 你是什么意思？

00:40:47.400 --> 00:40:49.980
<v Neil Drumming>It's hot! This is a hot meet-cute! This is hot!
<v Neil Drumming>这太火辣了！这种初次相遇太性感了！

00:40:49.980 --> 00:40:53.490
<v Emanuele Berry>It's a very good meet-cute.
<v Emanuele Berry>这是一个非常棒的相遇。

00:40:53.490 --> 00:40:54.840
<v George>Am I talking too much?
<v George>我话太多了吗？

00:40:54.840 --> 00:40:55.770
<v Man>Yes!
<v Man>是的！

00:40:55.770 --> 00:40:57.270
<v Man>[NEIL AND EMANUELE LAUGH]
<v Man>[笑]

00:40:57.270 --> 00:40:59.400
<v Man>Why don't you kiss her instead of talking her to death?
<v Man>你为什么不吻她，而不是一直说到死呢？

00:40:59.400 --> 00:41:00.420
<v George>How's that?
<v George>这样呢？

00:41:00.420 --> 00:41:02.640
<v Man>Why don't you kiss her, instead of talking her to death?
<v Man>为什么不吻她，而是说到死呢？

00:41:02.640 --> 00:41:06.120
<v Neil Drumming>This is wild sexy. This is amazing to me. This is so hot to me.
<v Neil Drumming>这太性感了。这让我非常震撼。对我来说这太火辣了。

00:41:06.120 --> 00:41:07.380
<v Neil Drumming>[EMANUELE LAUGHS]
<v Neil Drumming>[笑]

00:41:07.380 --> 00:41:10.150
<v Man>Oh! Youth is wasted on the wrong people.
<v Man>噢！青春真是浪费在错误的人身上了。

00:41:10.150 --> 00:41:12.410
<v Man>[LAUGHTER]
<v Man>[笑声]

00:41:12.410 --> 00:41:24.540
<v Emanuele Berry>The chemistry between the two leads on this fateful walk home-- I can tell Neil's at least somewhat sold. George and Mary get married. And just when George is about to get out of town, travel with his wife, there's a bank run.
<v Emanuele Berry>男女主角在回家的宿命之路上产生的化学反应——我能看出尼尔至少有点被打动了。乔治和玛丽结婚了。就在乔治即将离开小镇、与妻子旅行时，发生了银行挤兑。

00:41:24.540 --> 00:41:26.220
<v Emanuele Berry>Then, after that-- a war.
<v Emanuele Berry>在那之后——是战争。

00:41:26.220 --> 00:41:27.060
<v Man>Then came a war.
<v Man>接着是战争。

00:41:27.060 --> 00:41:49.530
<v Emanuele Berry>Each time, he's faced with another obstacle. Restuck, he sticks around, tries to fix it-- until one fateful Christmas Eve. His business is about to go under because of a careless mistake made by one of his employees, but George is the one who will be held responsible. He's lost, feels like he has no place to turn. He goes home. Mary and the kids are decorating for Christmas, and he snaps.
<v Emanuele Berry>每一次他都面临障碍。被困住，留下，试图修复——直到那个决定性的平安夜。他的生意因为员工的一个疏忽就要倒闭了，但责任人却是乔治。他迷失了，感到无路可走。他回家。玛丽和孩子们正在布置圣诞节，他崩溃了。

00:41:49.530 --> 00:41:50.700
<v Mary>George, what's wrong?
<v Mary>乔治，你怎么了？

00:41:50.700 --> 00:41:56.080
<v George>Wrong? Everything's wrong. You call this a happy family? Why do we have to have all these kids?
<v George>怎么了？一切都糟透了。你管这叫幸福家庭？为什么我们要生这么多孩子？

00:41:56.080 --> 00:41:59.730
<v Neil Drumming>Ooh! Damn!
<v Neil Drumming>噢！该死！

00:41:59.730 --> 00:42:03.120
<v Emanuele Berry>Not so Merry Christmas-y, is it, Neil?
<v Emanuele Berry>不像是个圣诞快乐的日子，对吧，尼尔？

00:42:03.120 --> 00:42:09.600
<v Neil Drumming>I'm with it. Let's get darker. Let go to see how dark this gets. I'm on board. Let's go darker. This is horrible.
<v Neil Drumming>我带入进去了。让我们更黑暗点吧。看看这能有多黑暗。我加入了。更黑暗点。这太可怕了。

00:42:09.600 --> 00:42:12.060
<v Neil Drumming>[EMANUELE LAUGHS]
<v Neil Drumming>[笑]

00:42:12.060 --> 00:42:16.560
<v Emanuele Berry>George wishes he'd never been born, and an angel named Clarence makes his wish come true.
<v Emanuele Berry>乔治许愿他从未出生过，天使克拉伦斯实现了他的愿望。

00:42:16.560 --> 00:42:17.520
<v Clarence>Is that your wish?
<v Clarence>那是你的愿望吗？

00:42:17.520 --> 00:42:25.020
<v Emanuele Berry>He sees what the town would be like if he didn't exist, and it's horrible-- ruled by money-grubbing robber barons. Nobody has jobs or hope.
<v Emanuele Berry>他看到了如果没有他，小镇会是什么样：那是可怕的——被贪婪的强盗大亨统治，没人有工作，没人有希望。

00:42:25.020 --> 00:42:26.370
<v Man>What's going on around here?
<v Man>这儿到底发生了什么？

00:42:26.370 --> 00:42:38.980
<v Emanuele Berry>Neil points out, it's very much like a film he has seen-- Back to the Future. Only then does George realize the impact his life has made and that he, in fact, had a wonderful life. Sorry, I couldn't help myself.
<v Emanuele Berry>尼尔指出，这和一部他看过的电影非常像——《回到未来》（Back to the Future）。只有在那时，乔治才意识到他的人生所产生的影响，以及他实际上拥有一个精彩的人生。抱歉，我忍不住。

00:42:38.980 --> 00:42:40.230
<v George>Help me, Clarence!
<v George>救救我，克拉伦斯！

00:42:40.230 --> 00:42:48.150
<v Emanuele Berry>Clarence takes back George's wish. He runs home to his family, and then everyone in town shows up and gives him money so his business will survive.
<v Emanuele Berry>克拉伦斯收回了乔治的愿望。他跑回家见家人，镇上所有人都赶来给了他钱，好让他的生意活下来。

00:42:48.150 --> 00:42:55.740
<v Man>Very nearly, George! Very nearly. She told some people you were in trouble. They scattered all over town, collecting money. Didn't ask any questions. Just heard George is in trouble and--
<v Man>真惊险，乔治！太惊险了。她告诉大家你遇到麻烦了。他们分散到镇上收集钱，没问任何问题。只是听说乔治有难——

00:42:55.740 --> 00:42:57.540
<v George>Well, these--
<v George>嗯，这些——

00:42:57.540 --> 00:43:00.090
<v Man 2>Another run on the bank?
<v Man 2>又一次银行挤兑？

00:43:00.090 --> 00:43:09.690
<v Emanuele Berry>I look over at Neil. No tears. But when the film ends, he tells me that he liked it, and he was surprised by it, and recognized that it was not just a Christmas film.
<v Emanuele Berry>我看向尼尔。没有眼泪。但当电影结束时，他告诉我他很喜欢，而且很惊讶，意识到这不仅仅是一部圣诞电影。

00:43:09.690 --> 00:43:18.300
<v Neil Drumming>I definitely forgot it was a Christmas movie. I stopped thinking about it as Christmas. It doesn't feel that-- like, that doesn't feel like what it's about. Until they sing.
<v Neil Drumming>我绝对忘了这是一部圣诞电影。我不再把它当成圣诞节电影来想。它给人的感觉——它讲的根本不是关于圣诞节的。直到他们唱起歌来。

00:43:18.300 --> 00:43:28.060
<v Emanuele Berry>I thought it was so funny when you were saying at the beginning that you were like, oh, yeah, I like Christmas movies where Christmas is in the background. I was like, yeah, this is one of those films, actually.
<v Emanuele Berry>我觉得很有意思的是，开头你说你喜欢背景里有圣诞节的电影，当时我就在想，嗯，其实这电影就是其中之一。

00:43:28.060 --> 00:43:43.650
<v Neil Drumming>Yeah, yeah. Even the "Auld Lang Syne" is, to me, a New Year's thing. It's like, the Christmas part-- it doesn't really come back to Christmas. It's not, like, about that.
<v Neil Drumming>是啊，是啊。就连《友谊地久天长》对我来说也是新年歌曲。所以圣诞节的部分——它并没有真正回到圣诞节。这不是它的核心。

00:43:43.650 --> 00:43:46.640
<v Neil Drumming>You know what it's like a lot? It's like Die Hard.
<v Neil Drumming>你知道它多像什么吗？它很像《虎胆龙威》。

00:43:46.640 --> 00:43:48.690
<v Neil Drumming>[LAUGHTER]
<v Neil Drumming>[笑声]

00:43:48.690 --> 00:43:51.240
<v Emanuele Berry>OK, OK. Say more.
<v Emanuele Berry>好吧，好吧。多说说。

00:43:51.240 --> 00:44:05.250
<v Neil Drumming>OK, they both-- the theme of both of them is to, like-- that things aren't as bad as you think. It's that your life could-- that if you stop and appreciate your life-- not even just appreciate. It's not just that you stop and appreciate your life.
<v Neil Drumming>它们俩的主题都是——事情并没有你想象的那么糟。这就是如果你停下来欣赏你的人生——甚至不仅仅是欣赏，不仅仅是停下来欣赏你的人生。

00:44:05.250 --> 00:44:25.830
<v Neil Drumming>It's that circumstance, dire circumstance, forces you to appreciate your life. So in Die Hard, like, a series of ridiculously absurd terrorist-type events makes it so that he appreciates his marriage and his family more. And he fights to save it. That's what I love about that movie.
<v Neil Drumming>而是说严峻的情况强迫你去欣赏你的人生。在《虎胆龙威》里，一系列荒谬的恐怖袭击事件让主角更加珍惜他的婚姻和家庭。他为之战斗。这就是我最喜欢那部电影的地方。

00:44:25.830 --> 00:44:34.350
<v Neil Drumming>That's literally the thing I love the most about that movie is how it is kind of a romantic film. Like, it's like, wait, everything I had was great. I should go back and save it.
<v Neil Drumming>我最喜欢的一点就是它其实是一部浪漫电影。就像是：等等，我拥有的一切都很棒。我应该回去拯救它。

00:44:34.350 --> 00:44:51.910
<v Neil Drumming>And this is that same thing of just, like, in the moment, when things are bad, you can feel like it wasn't worth it. But if you stop and look at it, it's worth saving. It's worth preserving.
<v Neil Drumming>这和那是一样的：在当下，事情很糟时，你会觉得不值得。但如果你停下来看看，它是值得拯救的，值得守护的。

00:44:51.910 --> 00:45:03.700
<v Neil Drumming>It's the end of the year. Like, maybe these kinds of movies are supposed to remind us that, like, amongst all the things you did wrong, there's something-- there's, like-- it's ultimately worth it. I don't know.
<v Neil Drumming>这是一年终了。也许这类电影是为了提醒我们，在你做错的所有事情中间，还是有某种东西……它们最终是值得的。我不知道。

00:45:03.700 --> 00:45:16.300
<v Neil Drumming>But maybe that's a common theme of, like, movies that air at the end of the year. Like, maybe we just want to be reassured, going into the next year, that what we did wasn't completely a waste of time.
<v Neil Drumming>但也许这是年末播放的电影的一个共同主题。也许在进入下一年之前，我们只是想要得到保证：我们所做的一切并非完全是在浪费时间。

00:45:16.300 --> 00:45:31.090
<v Emanuele Berry>At one point while we're talking about the film, Neil asked why I cry at that moment the townspeople come out to support George. And I say, it's nice to think that people might recognize all that you do for them. Neil tells me, maybe that's why I'm emotional-- because I do a lot for other people.
<v Emanuele Berry>当我们在讨论电影时，尼尔问我为什么镇民们出来支持乔治的那一刻我会哭。我说，想到人们可能会认可你为他们所做的一切，这种感觉真好。尼尔告诉我，也许这就是我为什么感性的原因——因为我为别人做了很多。

00:45:31.090 --> 00:45:54.790
<v Emanuele Berry>And Neil says maybe that's why he's not so emotional. Because, well, he doesn't. Not news to me. Case in point, what I did for him this Christmas-- gave him the gift of watching It's a Wonderful Life. What he did for me-- got me to watch Die Hard.
<v Emanuele Berry>尼尔说也许这就是他为什么没那么感性的原因。因为，嗯，他没怎么做。这对我来说不是新闻。举个例子，这个圣诞节我为他做了什么——给了他看《风云人物》的礼物。他为我做了什么——让我看了《虎胆龙威》。

00:45:54.790 --> 00:46:02.760
<v Ira Glass>Emanuele Berry is our show's executive editor.
<v Ira Glass>埃曼努埃尔·贝里是我们节目的执行编辑。

00:46:02.760 --> 00:46:14.540
<v Ira Glass>Act Four, Single Bells. Scaachi Koul is celebrating a Christmas first of her own this year, one that she is still puzzling through. Here she is.
<v Ira Glass>第四幕，单身铃声。斯卡奇·考尔（Scaachi Koul）今年也在庆祝她自己的第一个圣诞节，对此她还在苦思冥想。有请。

00:46:14.540 --> 00:46:31.270
<v Scaachi Koul>My marriage was over by last Christmas, but I didn't notice, because I was having such a nice time. One of my ex's many radiant qualities was how much he liked the holidays-- always in stark contrast to me, a misery gremlin who recently escaped a bog and is allergic to natural sunlight.
<v Scaachi Koul>去年的圣诞节时我的婚姻就已经结束了，但我没注意到，因为那时我过得很开心。我的前任拥有许多闪光品质，其中之一就是他有多么喜欢节日——这总是与我形成鲜明对比，我是一个刚从泥潭逃出来的、对自然阳光过敏的苦难怪兽。

00:46:31.270 --> 00:46:50.970
<v Scaachi Koul>I didn't really grow up celebrating Christmas. I was raised Hindu by immigrants who just didn't really care about the holiday. The extent of our decorations was a sparse plastic tree with screwed-in branches that looked like if a spruce had recently been set ablaze. Our family's dedication to the holiday was always cartoonishly pathetic.
<v Scaachi Koul>我小时候并不怎么过圣诞节。我是在移民家庭中长大的印度教徒，他们根本不在乎节日。我们的装饰程度仅仅是一棵稀疏的塑料树，那些插上去的树枝看起来像是云杉被烧过一样。我们家对节日的投入总是滑稽可怜。

00:46:50.970 --> 00:47:12.990
<v Scaachi Koul>But when I met my ex a little over 10 years ago, I realized that being with him meant being with a Christmas person. It was like joining a cult where the belief structure was built around a glazed ham. Being with him meant we bought a real, live tree, year after year, filling our apartment with pine needles that I'd find until we bought the next real, live tree.
<v Scaachi Koul>但十多年前遇到他时，我意识到和他在一起就意味着和“圣诞人”在一起。这就像加入了一个信仰结构建立在冰糖火腿之上的邪教。和他在一起意味着我们要年复一年地买一棵真正的活树，让我们的公寓里充满松针，直到我们买下一棵新的为止。

00:47:12.990 --> 00:47:32.160
<v Scaachi Koul>"Doesn't it smell great?" he'd say. And I'd reply with, no, because years of cigarette smoking has robbed me of a sense of smell. My ex-husband was practically competitive about gift-giving, always giving me the best gift, the thing I wanted, sometimes even the several things I wanted.
<v Scaachi Koul>“闻起来是不是很棒？”他会问。我会回答：“不，因为多年抽烟让我失去了嗅觉。”我的前夫在送礼物方面简直很有好胜心，总是给我最好的、我想要的，有时甚至是我想要的一大堆东西。

00:47:32.160 --> 00:47:51.390
<v Scaachi Koul>One year, he took me to Cuba. Another year, he bought me a Nintendo console that had been reliably sold out for months. The year after that-- delicate diamond studs for my non-deserving ears. I got him a bag, like, three times. Just three different bags.
<v Scaachi Koul>有一年他带我去了古巴。有一年他给我买了那款连续好几个月都卖断货的任天堂游戏机。后来那一年——给我那双不配戴钻石的小耳朵买了精致的耳钉。我呢？我送过他三次包，就是三个不同的包。

00:47:51.390 --> 00:48:04.320
<v Scaachi Koul>I don't know. You seem to need a lot of bags. I don't know what I'm doing out here. OK?
<v Scaachi Koul>我不知道。你看起来好像很需要包。我不知道我在这里做什么。好吗？

00:48:04.320 --> 00:48:27.030
<v Scaachi Koul>I resented Christmas and his love for it often. We used to fight about how I wanted to go see my family for the holidays, even though there wasn't much of a holiday to speak of, and how he wanted to go back to his sleepy little hometown with a three-car Santa Claus parade, and his stepmother's gingerbread village that the grandchildren bickered over, and the minced sauce with his dad's lamb, and the Christmas brunch with his mother.
<v Scaachi Koul>我经常怨恨圣诞节和他对圣诞节的热爱。我们过去常吵架，因为我过节想看望家人，尽管没什么可过的；而他想回到那个有三辆车参加圣诞老人游行、继母家孩子们争抢姜饼村、还有配羊排的酱汁、以及和他妈妈一起吃圣诞早午餐的小镇。

00:48:27.030 --> 00:48:50.110
<v Scaachi Koul>I was always fighting against my instincts to hate everything, to be cynical, to ask to be left alone. I didn't like Christmas because it required so much sincerity and emotional intelligence I clearly, obviously, still lack. We separated in February, and he's now off building a life I know nothing about.
<v Scaachi Koul>我总是在抵抗内心讨厌一切、玩世不恭、想要独处的本能。我不喜欢圣诞节，因为它需要太多的诚意和情商，而这显然是我依然欠缺的。我们在二月分开了，他现在过着我一无所知的生活。

00:48:50.110 --> 00:49:10.610
<v Scaachi Koul>I was worried about how he'd do, alone at Christmas. But you know who is alone, in actuality? Me, the Christmas hater. I'm Grinch at the top of the mountain, looking down at the denizens of Whoville, asking in the smallest voice possible, do they miss me down there?
<v Scaachi Koul>我曾担心圣诞节他一个人会怎样。但你知道实际上谁才是一个人吗？是我，这个圣诞仇恨者。我是站在山顶上往下看呼呼镇（Whoville）居民的格林奇，用极小的声音问道：他们想我吗？

00:49:10.610 --> 00:49:30.050
<v Scaachi Koul>Because though I was always loathe to admit it, I did like so many things about Christmas with his family. I did like how I always felt warm and soft between December 23 and 26, like I was a big, fat baby in a plush blanket. I became comfortable with it. And eventually, I learned to look forward to it.
<v Scaachi Koul>因为尽管我一直不愿承认，但我确实喜欢和他家人一起过圣诞节的许多方面。我喜欢在 12 月 23 日到 26 日之间那种温暖柔软的感觉，就像我是个被裹在毛绒毯子里的胖娃娃。我习惯了它，最终我开始期待它。

00:49:30.050 --> 00:49:47.090
<v Scaachi Koul>Christmas with him and his people was the closest to cortisol zero I've ever gotten in my life. My ex-husband's mother and stepmother always told me they loved me on Christmas Day when we exchanged gifts. And I always said it back. I meant it, too-- a rarity for me.
<v Scaachi Koul>和他及他家人一起度过的圣诞节，是我一生中皮质醇水平最低的时候。前夫的妈妈和继母总在圣诞节交换礼物时告诉我她们爱我。我也总是回敬。我是真心的——这对我是种稀罕事。

00:49:47.090 --> 00:50:23.830
<v Scaachi Koul>I liked being a daughter-in-law and offering to help with the turkey, even though I have no idea how to cook a turkey. There was comfort in opening the garage door and seeing his cousins piled in there, wearing old Labatt sweaters, chain-smoking, and fighting over who stole whose lighter-- all these 40-something-year-olds reverting to who they were when they were 17. My ex grew up somewhere without the pollution of a big city, so you could always see the stars. I wish I had spent more time looking at them.
<v Scaachi Koul>我喜欢作为儿媳妇去帮忙做火鸡，尽管我根本不知道怎么烤火鸡。车库门打开，看到他的堂兄弟们穿着旧毛衣、抽着烟、争论谁偷了谁的打火机——这种感觉很舒适，看着这些 40 多岁的人回归到 17 岁时的样子。我的前任在没有大城市污染的地方长大，所以总能看到星星。我真希望我当时花了更多时间去看它们。

00:50:23.830 --> 00:50:42.220
<v Scaachi Koul>To marry into a family that loves Christmas is to be conscripted into something that feels pleasingly conspiratorial. You know exactly what you need to do, and where, and for whom. The days pass and inch you closer to December 24, and you have a precise awareness of what's expected of and for you.
<v Scaachi Koul>嫁给一个热爱圣诞节的家庭，意味着被征召进某种令人愉悦的共谋中。你知道你需要做什么、在哪里做、为谁做。日子一天天过去，离 12 月 24 日越来越近，你清楚地知道对你有什么期望。

00:50:42.220 --> 00:51:01.790
<v Scaachi Koul>And when the holiday is done, you have all these little secrets to whisper to each other in delight. "Remember that year my mom put brown sugar in the eggs she made for Christmas Day brunch?" my husband would whisper into my mouth while we lay in bed, after we returned to our lives in New York, to the albatross that increasingly was our marriage.
<v Scaachi Koul>而当节日结束时，你们有一些小秘密可以窃窃私语：“还记得我妈在圣诞早午餐做的鸡蛋里加了红糖那年吗？”我丈夫会在我们回到纽约、躺在床上后，对着我的嘴唇低语，那时我们的婚姻已经成了越来越重的负担。

00:51:01.790 --> 00:51:14.600
<v Scaachi Koul>I'd laugh and say, "Yes, but we ate it. Because we love her, and we love each other, right? We love each other. Say it one more time, just so I'm sure that you're sure."
<v Scaachi Koul>我会大笑说：“是啊，但我们吃掉了。因为我们爱她，我们也爱彼此，对吧？我们爱彼此。再说一遍，让我确定你确定。”

00:51:14.600 --> 00:51:26.770
<v Scaachi Koul>Now, all I feel is this constant twinge in my gut. It's like having a tail cut off. I wasn't supposed to even have one. But I did for a while. And now, I don't.
<v Scaachi Koul>现在，我感觉到胃里有一种持续的抽搐。就像被砍掉了尾巴。我本来不该有那条尾巴的，但我曾拥有过一段时间。而现在，我没有了。

00:51:26.770 --> 00:51:43.300
<v Scaachi Koul>I thought about spending Christmas alone this year. I could do something devastating and romantic, like take myself on a solo vacation, maybe somewhere sunny, where I'd swan around in drapey fabric and look sad and hot and sad. Maybe at a resort.
<v Scaachi Koul>我曾想今年圣诞节独自一人。我可以做些毁灭性但浪漫的事，比如独自去度假，也许去个阳光充足的地方，穿着垂坠的布料四处漫步，看起来既忧郁又性感。也许在度假村里。

00:51:43.300 --> 00:52:08.890
<v Scaachi Koul>Everyone there would think I had a secret. But the secret is, I'm sad. Instead, on both hands, I count the people I won't speak to this December. His folks, his siblings, his nieces and nephews, the cousin of his that was my favorite, the other cousin of his who always spent too much time touching my lower back, his aunt, who always called me Sasha, even 10 years in.
<v Scaachi Koul>每个人都会以为我有什么秘密。但秘密是，我很难过。相反，我数着这个十二月我不会交谈的人：他的父母、他的兄弟姐妹、他的侄甥辈，我最喜欢的那位堂兄弟，那个总是喜欢触碰我后腰的堂兄弟，还有总是叫我“萨莎”的姑妈，即便认识十年了还是这样。

00:52:08.890 --> 00:52:39.460
<v Scaachi Koul>I'll add this pain to the several others that hardened me from the inside-out. The first year of divorce is just a march of agonies. Every notable event-- the first one you do by yourself. First birthday where we don't even speak, first family medical crisis where we don't cling to each other like driftwood in a tsunami, and now, our first Christmas apart.
<v Scaachi Koul>我将把这份痛苦加到其他让我内心变硬的经历中。离婚的第一年简直是一场痛苦的行军。每一个重要的事件——都是你第一次独自面对。第一个我们甚至不说话的生日，第一个我们不会像海啸中的浮木一样紧紧相拥的家庭医疗危机，现在，我们分开后的第一个圣诞节。

00:52:39.460 --> 00:52:59.360
<v Scaachi Koul>My grief around my divorce remains wildly unpredictable. I don't have any strategy or plan on how to cope with the holidays. I just have to walk through a TJ Maxx and glare at the Christmas display and how I have no use for a Santa Claus cookie jar. I never did, but now, I really don't.
<v Scaachi Koul>我对离婚的悲伤依旧完全无法预测。对于如何度过假期，我没有任何策略或计划。我只需要走进 TJ Maxx，怒视着那些圣诞陈列品，感叹我为什么需要一个圣诞老人的饼干罐。我以前从来不需要，但现在，我绝对不需要。

00:52:59.360 --> 00:53:19.970
<v Scaachi Koul>In the end, I decided to just go home this year and lick my wounds amongst my own family. My 12-year-old niece and sister-in-law celebrate Christmas with glee. There are cookies to be iced, and an embarrassment of riches under the tree, and a fireplace, and a mini dachshund named Beans, who will gladly snap a hunk of turkey off of your fork.
<v Scaachi Koul>最后，我决定今年回家，在自己的家庭中舔舐伤口。我 12 岁的侄女和嫂子欢天喜地地庆祝圣诞节。有饼干要抹糖霜，树下有数不尽的礼物，有壁炉，还有一只叫“豆子”（Beans）的迷你腊肠犬，它会乐意从你的叉子上抢走一块火鸡。

00:53:19.970 --> 00:53:36.980
<v Scaachi Koul>Even my dad enjoys Christmas, if forced. He will never say no to a slice of pecan pie after a dinner that he complains does not have enough masala. I could conceivably find pleasure in this holiday again if I worked at it. But I don't want to right now.
<v Scaachi Koul>连我爸如果被强迫的话，也会享受圣诞节。他在抱怨晚餐里的马萨拉（香料）不够多之后，永远不会拒绝一片碧根果派。如果我愿意努力，我本可以在这个节日里再次找到快乐。但我现在不想。

00:53:36.980 --> 00:53:56.090
<v Scaachi Koul>It's not that I like or dislike the holiday. It's simply that now it hurts my feelings. I crave nothing more than indifference, to view Christmas as but another holiday that isn't mine-- Easter, Hanukkah, Eid. You won't see me crying over Casimir Pulaski Day.
<v Scaachi Koul>并不是我喜欢或不喜欢这个节日，只是现在它触动了我的伤心事。我只想感到漠不关心，把圣诞节看作另一个不属于我的节日——复活节、光明节、开斋节。你不会看到我为卡齐米日·普瓦斯基日哭泣。

00:53:56.090 --> 00:54:15.250
<v Scaachi Koul>For the last year, everything has felt either triumphant or devastating. And it would be nice to feel, instead, nothing at all.
<v Scaachi Koul>过去一年里，一切都感觉要么是凯旋，要么是毁灭。如果能感觉什么都没有，那该多好。

00:54:15.250 --> 00:55:02.410
<v Ira Glass>Scaachi Cole is the author of One Day We'll All Be Dead and None of This Will Matter, and a cultural reporter at BuzzFeed News. Her story was produced by Diane Wu.
<v Ira Glass>斯卡奇·考尔是《总有一天我们都会死，而这一切都不重要》的作者，也是 BuzzFeed News 的文化记者。她的故事由黛安·吴（Diane Wu）制作。

00:55:02.410 --> 00:55:19.060
